\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), these are precisely the admissible values.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on the chosen lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound  \n\nIf we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, at most all of them can be sunny; therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), hence it is **not** sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in this range, so it lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=2}^{n+1}\\).  \nThus the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\{L_c\\}\\) cover all points of \\(T_n\\) and we have a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le n)\\)\n\n##### 3.1  Choice of sunny lines  \n\nSelect the \\(k\\) points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k) .\n\\]\n\nAll these points satisfy \\(i+(n+1-i)=n+1\\); they lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\) choose a real number \\(\\alpha_i\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad \\alpha_i\\neq\\alpha_j\\;(j\\neq i).\n\\]\n\nBecause the set \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) is infinite, such a choice is possible.  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\alpha_i\\bigl(x-i\\bigr) .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) passes through \\(P_i\\), has slope \\(\\alpha_i\\) (hence is sunny), and the \\(S_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n##### 3.2  Choice of the remaining lines  \n\nConsider the family of diagonals  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1),\\;c\\neq n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n-k\\) such lines, because the value \\(c=n+1\\) has been omitted.\n\nAll these lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); consequently they are not sunny.\n\n##### 3.3  Verification of the covering property  \n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b\\neq n+1\\)*, then \\(c:=a+b\\) belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{n+1\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(L_c\\).\n\n*If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, then \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\); by construction \\(P_i\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\n\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\neq n+1\\},\n\\]\nand exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\), the set of admissible values is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
