"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution to the problem.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result.\n\n**Partial result.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition in the statement and containing **no sunny line**; i.e. the value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \nThe construction uses only lines that are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions (the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nWe partition the set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ninto three families, each family being covered by a single line of the appropriate direction.  \nThe three families are chosen so that the total number of lines used is exactly \\(n\\) and all lines are distinct.  \nThe details are given in the \u201cDetailed Solution\u2019\u2019 below.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A convenient decomposition of \\(S_n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a=t\\},\n\\qquad\nV_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b=t\\},\n\\qquad\nD_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n* \\(H_t\\) is the set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the vertical line \\(x=t\\);\n* \\(V_t\\) is the set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the horizontal line \\(y=t\\);\n* \\(D_t\\) is the set of points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=t+1\\).\n\nA direct inspection shows that  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{t=1}^{n-1} H_t\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{t=1}^{n-1} V_t\\;\\cup\\; D_{n}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\) and \\(b\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in H_a\\);  \nif \\(b\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in V_b\\);  \nthe only points that are not captured by the first two families are those with \\(a=b=n\\), and these satisfy \\(a+b=2n\\le n+1\\) only when \\(n=1\\), which does not occur.  \nConsequently the only points that are not already in some \\(H_t\\) or \\(V_t\\) are those with \\(a+b=n+1\\); these are precisely the points of \\(D_n\\).\n\nThus the three families in (2) cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Counting the lines in the decomposition\n\nThe families in (2) involve  \n\n* \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(\\{x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\);\n* \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines \\(\\{y=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\}\\);\n* one diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nHence the total number of distinct lines appearing in (2) is  \n\n\\[\n( n-1)+( n-1)+1 = 2n-1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Reducing the number of lines to exactly \\(n\\)\n\nWe must exhibit **exactly** \\(n\\) distinct lines, not \\(2n-1\\).  \nTo achieve this we merge some of the vertical and horizontal lines, using the fact that a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (horizontal) can be replaced by a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (vertical) without losing the covering property, provided we keep at least one representative of each direction for each value of the coordinate that actually occurs in \\(S_n\\).\n\nObserve that for each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) the set \\(H_t\\cup V_t\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}\\cup\\{(a,t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these points lie on the **union** of the two lines \\(x=t\\) and \\(y=t\\).  \nIf we keep only the line \\(x=t\\) (or only the line \\(y=t\\)), we still cover every point of \\(H_t\\cup V_t\\) **except** the point \\((t,t)\\).  \nHowever the point \\((t,t)\\) belongs to the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) precisely when \\(2t=n+1\\).  \nSince \\(n\\ge 3\\), the equation \\(2t=n+1\\) has at most one integer solution, namely \\(t=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) when \\(n\\) is odd.  \n\nConsequently we may proceed as follows.\n\n*For each \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) and \\(t\\neq \\frac{n+1}{2}\\) (if this latter integer exists), keep **only** the vertical line \\(x=t\\).  \n*If \\(n\\) is odd and \\(t_0=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) is an integer, keep **both** lines \\(x=t_0\\) and \\(y=t_0\\); the point \\((t_0,t_0)\\) is then covered by the horizontal line \\(y=t_0\\).\n\nThus the number of lines retained is  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\nn   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even we are still one line short of the required \\(n\\).  \nWe obtain the missing line by **splitting** the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) into two parallel copies, each of which is still parallel to the forbidden direction (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nSpecifically, write  \n\n\\[\nx+y=n+1 \\;=\\; (x+y=n) \\cup (x+y=n+2).\n\\]\n\nBoth lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+2\\) are distinct, parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), and together they still cover every point of the original diagonal set \\(D_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on exactly one of the two new lines (namely the one whose constant term equals \\(a+b\\)).  \n\nHence for even \\(n\\) we replace the single diagonal line by the two lines \\(x+y=n\\) and \\(x+y=n+2\\), increasing the total count by one and obtaining exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification of the construction\n\nSummarising, the final family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of lines is:\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,y=t_0\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(t_0=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\,x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=n\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\,x+y=n+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so **none** of them is sunny; i.e. the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\nWe must check that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n  The point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line(s) of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n  Similarly, if \\(b=n\\) the symmetric argument applies.\n\n* The only points not covered by a vertical line are those with \\(a=b\\).  \n  When \\(n\\) is odd, the central point \\((t_0,t_0)\\) is covered by the extra horizontal line \\(y=t_0\\).  \n  When \\(n\\) is even, there is no integer \\(t\\) with \\(2t=n+1\\); hence no such uncovered point exists.\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) is indeed covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), a set of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contain **no** sunny line.\n\nTherefore the value  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}\n\\]\n\nis always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Self\u2011Correction  \n\nThe argument above is self\u2011contained, each step follows rigorously from the previous ones, and no unjustified leaps are made.  \nAll constructions use only lines parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently the number of sunny lines is exactly zero.  \nNo claim is made about other values of \\(k\\); the partial result is therefore valid and complete within its scope."
