Thus (1) yields a covering of \\(S_n\\) by  \n\n\\[\n( n-1)+( n-1)+1 = 2n-1\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\ndistinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Reducing the number of lines to exactly \\(n\\)\n\nWe must replace the family of \\(2n-1\\) lines by a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covering \\(S_n\\) and still non‑sunny.\n\nObserve that for each \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) the union \\(V_t\\cup H_t\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}\\cup\\{(a,t)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we keep **only** the vertical line \\(x=t\\), every point of \\(V_t\\) is still covered, while the points of \\(H_t\\) are covered except possibly the point \\((t,t)\\).  \nThe point \\((t,t)\\) belongs to the diagonal line \\(D_n\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\n2t=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3) has at most one integer solution; it occurs precisely when \\(n\\) is odd, in which case the solution is  \n\n\\[\nt_0=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently we can proceed as follows.\n\n*For every \\(t\\neq t_0\\) (if \\(t_0\\) exists) keep only the vertical line \\(x=t\\).  \n*If \\(n\\) is odd, retain additionally the horizontal line \\(y=t_0\\) so that the point \\((t_0,t_0)\\) is covered.  \n*If \\(n\\) is even, no integer satisfies (3); therefore keeping only the vertical lines suffices.\n\nAfter this step we have  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{lines}= \n\\begin{cases}\nn-1 &\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\nn   &\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we already have exactly \\(n\\) lines (the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines, the extra horizontal line, and the diagonal line \\(D_n\\)).  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even we are one line short of the required number.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completing the even case  \n\nFor even \\(n\\) we replace the single diagonal line \\(D_n\\) by **two** distinct lines parallel to it:\n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x+y=n,\\qquad L_2:\\;x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\) are parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and are distinct from each other and from all vertical lines \\(x=t\\).  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\) lies on exactly one of \\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\) (namely on the one whose constant term equals \\(a+b\\)).  \nThus the set \\(D_n\\) is still covered, now by two lines instead of one, and the total number of lines becomes  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+2 = n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Verification of the final families  \n\nSummarising, the required family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines is:\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\n\\{\\,x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=t_0\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\},\n\\qquad t_0=\\frac{n+1}{2}.\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\n\\{\\,x=t\\mid 1\\le t\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,x+y=n+2\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence **none** of them is sunny; the number of sunny lines is therefore \\(k=0\\).\n\nWe must check that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_n\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n  The point \\((n,1)\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\) and therefore lies on the diagonal line(s) of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).  \n  The symmetric situation \\(b=n\\) is handled analogously.\n* The only points not covered by a vertical line are those with \\(a=b\\).  \n  When \\(n\\) is odd, the unique such point that could be missed is \\((t_0,t_0)\\); it is covered by the extra horizontal line \\(y=t_0\\).  \n  When \\(n\\) is even, equation \\(2a=n+1\\) has no integer solution, so no such point exis