"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a complete solution of the problem.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following partial result.\n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\). If a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with\n> \\(a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), then at least one of the lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be sunny.\n\nConsequently, the value \\(k=0\\) (i.e. \u201call the lines are non\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019) is **impossible** for every admissible \\(n\\).\n\nThe remaining values of \\(k\\) (that is, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\)) are not settled by the argument below; the problem of deciding which of them actually occur is left open.\n\n---\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\nThe proof proceeds by contradiction.  \nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, i.e. each line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions:\nthe \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThus each line belongs to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\n\nwith distinct constants \\(c\\).  \n\nCounting the lattice points that can lie on a line of each family gives the following\nupper bounds:\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c,n+1-c\\}\\) points of the required set,\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c,n+1-c\\}\\) points,\n* a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) can contain at most \\(\\min\\{c-1,\\,n+2-c\\}\\) points.\n\nSumming these bounds over all lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) yields a total that is **strictly smaller**\nthan the total number of points that have to be covered, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence a covering with only non\u2011sunny lines is impossible, establishing the lemma.\n\nThe argument uses only elementary counting and the fact that the three families are pairwise\nnon\u2011parallel, which forces the constants that define the lines to be distinct.  No use is made\nof any sophisticated geometry; the contradiction follows from a simple inequality.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\);\n2. none of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny, i.e. each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nConsequently each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) belongs to exactly one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the lines are distinct, the constants that appear are all different; denote\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=h_{1},\\dots ,y=h_{p}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=v_{1},\\dots ,x=v_{q}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=d_{1},\\dots ,x+y=d_{r}\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(p+q+r=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 1.  How many required points can lie on a given line?\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) is the line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\), then the points of \\(S\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are those with\n\\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\).  Since \\(b\\) must be a positive integer, \\(c\\) itself must be a positive integer.\nHence the number of points of \\(S\\) on \\(y=c\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\{a\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a\\le n+1-c\\}= \\max\\{0,\\,n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause also \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(c\\ge 1\\), we obtain the sharper bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{y=c\\})\\le \\min\\{c,\\,n+1-c\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nThe same reasoning for a line \\(x=c\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{x=c\\})\\le \\min\\{c,\\,n+1-c\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nFor a line \\(x+y=d\\) with integer \\(d\\ge 2\\) (otherwise it contains no point of \\(S\\)),\nthe condition \\(a+b=d\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and\n\\(1\\le b\\le d-1\\).  Moreover we need \\(d\\le n+1\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{x+y=d\\})=\n\\begin{cases}\nd-1, & 2\\le d\\le \\frac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2mm]\nn+2-d, & \\frac{n+2}{2}< d\\le n+1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nwhich can be written compactly as  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{x+y=d\\})\\le \\min\\{d-1,\\,n+2-d\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Summing the contributions\n\nUsing (2)\u2013(4) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\#(S\\cap\\ell_{i})\n&\\le\n\\sum_{j=1}^{p}\\min\\{h_{j},\\,n+1-h_{j}\\}\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\min\\{v_{j},\\,n+1-v_{j}\\}\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{r}\\min\\{d_{j}-1,\\,n+2-d_{j}\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll constants \\(h_{j},v_{j},d_{j}\\) are distinct positive integers not exceeding \\(n+1\\).\nConsequently each term in the three sums is at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\).\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) terms altogether, the right\u2013hand side of (5) is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor.\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|, \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\) with strict inequality when \\(n\\) is odd,\nand equality when \\(n\\) is even gives \\(n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\nThus the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can possibly lie on the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines is **strictly smaller** than \\(|S|\\).  Hence the set \\(S\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) lines all of which are non\u20