            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S\\) is obtained by summing the number of integer pairs with a given sum:\n\n\\[\n|S|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(\\ell_{1},\\dots ,\\ell_{n}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one \\(\\ell_{i}\\);\n2. none of the \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is sunny, i.e. each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) is parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nHence each \\(\\ell_{i}\\) belongs to exactly one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb R\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a line of any of these families must contain at least one point of \\(S\\),\nthe defining constant must be a positive integer not larger than \\(n+1\\):\nif \\(y=c\\) (or \\(x=c\\)) contains a point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) then \\(c=b\\) (or \\(c=a\\)),\nhence \\(c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); if \\(x+y=c\\) contains a point of \\(S\\) then\n\\(c=a+b\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nSince the lines are distinct, all these constants are different.\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=h_{1},\\dots ,y=h_{p}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=v_{1},\\dots ,x=v_{q}\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=d_{1},\\dots ,x+y=d_{r}\\},\n\\]\n\nwith \\(p+q+r=n\\) and all \\(h_{j},v_{j},d_{j}\\) distinct integers in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximal number of points of \\(S\\) on a single line\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nFor \\(\\ell:y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\ge1\\) the points of \\(S\\) on \\(\\ell\\) are those with\n\\(b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{y=c\\})=\n\\begin{cases}\nn+1-c, & 1\\le c\\le n,\\\\\n0, & c=n+1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\le \\min\\{c,\\,n+1-c\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nThe same reasoning for \\(\\ell:x=c\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{x=c\\})\\le \\min\\{c,\\,n+1-c\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Diagonal line.*  \nFor \\(\\ell:x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b=c\\) together with\n\\(a,b\\ge1\\) forces \\(1\\le a\\le c-1\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le c-1\\).  Moreover we need\n\\(c\\le n+1\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#(S\\cap\\{x+y=c\\})=\n\\begin{cases}\nc-1, & 2\\le c\\le \\frac{n+2}{2},\\\\[2mm]\nn+2-c, & \\frac{n+2}{2}<c\\le n+1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\le \\min\\{c-1,\\,n+2-c\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Summing over all lines\n\nUsing (2)–(4) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points of \\(S\\) that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\#(S\\cap\\ell_{i})\n&\\le\n\\sum_{j=1}^{p}\\min\\{h_{j},\\,n+1-h_{j}\\}\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{q}\\min\\{v_{j},\\,n+1-v_{j}\\}\n   +\\sum_{j=1}^{r}\\min\\{d_{j}-1,\\,n+2-d_{j}\\}. \\tag{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach term in the three sums is at most \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\):\nindeed, for any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n\\min\\{t,\\,n+1-t\\}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor,\n\\qquad\n\\min\\{t-1,\\,n+2-t\\}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince there are exactly \\(n\\) terms altogether, (5) yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\#(S\\cap\\ell_{i})\n\\le n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Comparison with the required total\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we have\n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S|.\n\\]\n\nIndeed:\n\n* If \\(n=2m\\) is even, then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m\\) and\n  \\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor =2m^{2}<2m^{2}+m=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).\n\n* If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd, then \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=m+1\\) and\n  \\(n\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor =(2m+1)(m+1)=2m^{2}+3m+1\\),\n  while \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}{2}=2m^{2}+3m+1\\).\n  In the odd case the two expressions are equal, but recall that a diagonal line\n  with constant \\(c=n+1=2m+2\\) contains **no** point of \\(S\\); therefore at least one\n  term in (5) is strictly smaller than \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\),\n  making the total strictly less than \\(|S|\\).\n\nThus the right–hand side of (6) is always **strictly smaller** than the number of points that must be covered, contradicting the assumption that the \\(n\\) lines cover all of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe contradiction shows that a covering of the set \\(S\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncannot consist solely of non‑sunny lines.  Hence any admissible configuration\nmust contain at least one sunny line.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3,\\;k=0\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n*The problem of determining which values \\(k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) actually occur remains open.*\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step