ny, covering the whole diagonal),  \n   * the \\(n-1-k\\) remaining lines \\(L_c\\) with \\(c\\notin C\\) (non‑sunny),  \n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained from Lemma 2.\n\n   All lines are distinct, there are exactly \\(n\\) of them, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them:\n   * points of the diagonal are on \\(L_0\\);\n   * a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\le n\\) and \\(c\\notin C\\) lies on the unchanged line \\(L_c\\);\n   * if \\(c\\in C\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line through \\(P_c\\) (because \\(P_c=(c-1,1)\\) belongs to that line and the line also contains the whole set of points with sum \\(c\\) – a direct computation shows that the equation of the sunny line is \\(y = (n-c)(x-(c-1))+1\\), which indeed reduces to \\(x+y=c\\) when \\(x=c-1\\)). Hence every point with sum \\(c\\) is covered.\n\n   Therefore a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   To see that **more** sunny lines are impossible, suppose a configuration contained \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Then only one non‑sunny line would remain.  \n   By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of the diagonal, leaving at least one diagonal point uncovered.  \n   The single non‑sunny line can be of slope \\(-1\\) (covering all diagonal points) **or** of slope \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\).  \n   If it is of slope \\(-1\\) it coincides with \\(L_0\\) and therefore cannot be distinct from the sunny lines (which are not allowed to have slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   If it is horizontal or vertical, it meets the diagonal in at most one point, leaving the other diagonal points uncovered.  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot satisfy the covering requirement.  \n   Consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion** – Combining the construction (showing that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable) with the impossibility of \\(k\\ge n-1\\) yields the exact set of feasible n