ntains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(D\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) points of \\(D\\) must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \nA non‑sunny line can contain more than one point of \\(D\\) only if it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nhas this slope and contains **all** points of \\(D\\). Consequently at most one non‑sunny line can be used for the diagonal, and the other non‑sunny lines must be horizontal or vertical.\n\nAfter removing the diagonal, the set that still has to be covered is  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\setminus D=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_c\\) have slope \\(-1\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of (11) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_c\\) (namely the one with \\(c=a+b\\)).  \nThus the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines and covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with **no** sunny line exists.\n\nNow suppose a configuration contained \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines.  \nThen at most one non‑sunny line would remain.  \nBy Lemma 1 the sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) diagonal points, leaving at least one diagonal point uncovered.  \nA single non‑sunny line can be horizontal, vertical, or parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nIf it is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) it coincides with \\(L_0\\) and therefore is not non‑sunny; if it is horizontal or vertical it meets the diagonal in at most one point, so some diagonal point stays uncovered.  \nThus a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) sunny lines is impossible. ∎  \n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nC\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad |C|=k .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(c\\in C\\) define the **sunny** line  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\;y=(n-c)\\bigl(x-(c-1)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n(Equation (16) is exactly the line through the two points \\(P_c=(c-1,1)\\) and \\(Q_c=(c,n+1-c)\\); see Lemma 2 below.)  \nAlso define the **vertical** line  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nNow form the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\n\\Bigl\\{L_0\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{L_n\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{L_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus C\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{S_c\\mid c\\in C\\Bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{V_c\\mid c\\in C\\Bigr\\}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\n**Counting.**  \nThe sets in (18) contain respectively  \n\n* 1 line \\(L_0\\);  \n* 1 line \\(L_n\\);  \n* \\((n-2)-k\\) lines \\(L_c\\) (the unchanged ones);  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_c\\);  \n* \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_c\\).\n\nHence \\(|\\mathcal F|=1+1+(n-2-k)+k+k=n\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct: the \\(L_c\\)’s have slope \\(-1\\); the \\(S_c\\)’s have slopes \\(n-c\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\); the \\(V_c\\)’s are vertical; and the two special lines \\(L_0\\) and \\(L_n\\) have slopes \\(-1\\) but different intercepts, so they differ from each other and from the vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 2.** For every \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the line \\(S_c\\) defined by (16) is sunny, passes through \\(P_c=(c-1,1)\\) and through the diagonal point \\(Q_c=(c,n+1-c)\\).\n\n*Proof.* The two points are distinct, so (16) is the unique line through them. Its slope equals \\(n-c\\). Because \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) we have \\(1\\le n-c\\le n-2\\); thus the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\). Hence the line is sunny. ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Coverage verification.**  \