of \\(C_1\\) requires at least \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nSince we have only \\(k\\) sunny lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\frac{n+1}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nIf we take all \\(n\\) lines vertical, then \\(k=0\\) and the condition is still satisfied.  \nThus any admissible \\(k\\) must obey  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (11) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThese are not sunny and they cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x-(n-k+i)+i .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(+1\\); consequently none of them is parallel to any of the three lines in (6).  \nHence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\n#### 2.3  Verification that the sunny lines cover the remaining points\n\nFix a column \\(C_j\\) as in (8).  \nFor any \\(i\\le j\\le k+1-i\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{point on }L_i\\text{ with }x=n-k+j\n&\\Longrightarrow y = (n-k+j)-(n-k+i)+i = j .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((n-k+j,\\,j)\\).  \nBecause of (9) the column \\(C_j\\) contains exactly the points \\((n-k+j,\\,b)\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le k+1-j .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe inequality \\(i\\le j\\le k+1-i\\) is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le j\\le k,\\qquad i\\le j\\le k+1-i .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nHence for every admissible \\(b\\) in (15) we can choose \\(i=j-b+1\\); this integer satisfies (16) and therefore the point \\((n-k+j,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,j-b+1}\\).\n\nConsequently **every** point of each column \\(C_j\\) is hit by exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nTogether with the vertical lines (12) all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny}) = n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have shown\n\n* the inequality (11) is necessary, and  \n* for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (11) an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones covering all points of \\(T_n\\) exists.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{ or }\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
