\\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nChoose the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}:=\\{x+y=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such diagonals, and they are pairwise distinct.  \nSince each point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\), the set \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) already covers **all** points of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(n\\) lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) form a covering of \\(S_{n}\\) that contains **no** sunny lines. Hence\n\n\\[\nk=0\\quad\\text{is attainable.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one\n\nFix a line \\(\\ell\\) from \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  \nSuppose \\(\\ell\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nThe points of \\(S_{n}\\) lying on \\(\\ell\\) are\n\n\\[\nP_{\\ell}:=\\{(a,c-a)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick any two distinct points of \\(P_{\\ell}\\), say \\((a_{1},c-a_{1})\\) and \\((a_{2},c-a_{2})\\) with \\(a_{1}\\neq a_{2}\\).  \nThe line \\(\\ell'\\) through these two points has slope\n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{(c-a_{2})-(c-a_{1})}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=\\frac{a_{1}-a_{2}}{a_{2}-a_{1}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(\\ell'\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) again.  \nTo obtain a **sunny** line we must choose the two points so that they are *not* collinear with slope \\(-1\\).  \nThis is achieved by taking one point from \\(P_{\\ell}\\) and one point from a different diagonal.\n\nLet us instead replace a vertical line \\(x=c\\) (which is not present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) but will be needed later) by a sunny line.  \nThe points of \\(S_{n}\\) on \\(x=c\\) are\n\n\\[\nQ_{c}:=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\nPick the two extreme points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((c,n+1-c)\\).  \nThe line through them has slope\n\n\\[\nm_{c}= \\frac{(n+1-c)-1}{c-c}= \\infty,\n\\]\n\ni.e. it is the original vertical line.  \nTo obtain a sunny line we perturb one of the points slightly: choose \\((c,