he replacement.  What we need is that every point of \\(S_{n}\\) remains covered by at least one line.\n\nObserve that the only points of \\(S_{n}\\) that were covered **exclusively** by the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) are the points of \\(P_{c}\\).  After we delete \\(D_{c}\\) we must ensure that each of those points is still covered.  To achieve this we replace \\(D_{c}\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c,t}\\) **and** we keep the diagonal \\(D_{c+1}\\) (which exists because \\(c+1\\le n+1\\)).  The diagonal \\(D_{c+1}\\) contains all points \\((a,c+1-a)\\) with \\(a=1,\\dots ,c\\); in particular it contains the point \\((c,1)\\), which is the last point of \\(P_{c}\\).  Similarly the diagonal \\(D_{c-1}\\) (when \\(c>2\\)) contains the point \\((1,c-1)\\).  Consequently every point of \\(P_{c}\\) is still covered either by \\(\\ell_{c,t}\\) (the two extreme points \\(A_{c}\\) and \\(B_{c}\\) are still present on the neighbouring diagonals) or by one of the adjacent diagonals that we have not removed.\n\nThus we may **safely delete** the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) and **add** the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c,t}\\); the covering of \\(S_{n}\\) is preserved and the number of sunny lines increases by exactly one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nStart from the non\u2011sunny covering  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}=\\{D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) perform the following procedure \\(k\\) times:\n\n* Choose a diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) that has not yet been replaced.\n* Replace it by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c,t}\\) constructed in \u00a72 (with any admissible \\(t\\)).\n\nEach replacement removes one non\u2011sunny line and adds one sunny line, while the covering property is maintained, as proved in \u00a72.  After exactly \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* still covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\)