t together cover a point that lies on a line of a different family.  \n   A simple counting argument (Lemma 3) shows that at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary to cover all points of \\(T_n\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction for every \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n\n   *Choose the points to be covered by sunny lines.*  \n   Take the \\(k\\) points on the hypotenuse  \n\n   \\[\n   P_t=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\qquad(t=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 pick for each \\(t\\) a sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) through \\(P_t\\) that contains no other point of \\(T_n\\); the \\(\\ell_t\\) are distinct.\n\n   *Cover the remaining points.*  \n   After deleting the \\(k\\) points \\(\\{P_t\\}\\) the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U=T_n\\setminus\\{P_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\n   \\]\n\n   still contains all points with \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   Every point of \\(U\\) lies on a horizontal line of \\(\\mathcal H\\) and on a vertical line of \\(\\mathcal V\\).  \n   Choose **one** horizontal line \\(y=c_0\\) with \\(c_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and **one** vertical line \\(x=d_0\\) with \\(d_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   c_0\\neq n+1-d_0 .\n   \\]\n\n   (Such a pair exists because \\(k\\le n-2\\) leaves at least two distinct values among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).)  \n   The two lines \\(y=c_0\\) and \\(x=d_0\\) together cover every point of \\(U\\): a point \\((a,b)\\in U\\) either has \\(b=c_0\\) or \\(a=d_0\\); if neither holds then \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces \\(b=n+1-a\\neq c_0\\) and \\(a\\neq d_0\\), contradicting the choice of \\(c_0,d_0\\).  \n\n   Thus the final family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{\\ell_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=c_0,\\;x=d_0\\,\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k+2=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 3 shows that larger \\(k\\) are impossible. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Three covering families)  \nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in H_b,\\qquad (a,b)\\in V_a,\\qquad (a,b)\\in D_{a+b},\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nH_c:\\;y=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad\nV_c:\\;x=c\\;(1\\le c\\le n),\\qquad\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\;(2\\le c\\le n+1).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) we may set \\(c=b\\) and obtain the horizontal line \\(y=c\\).  \nSimilarly \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) gives the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nFinally \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\) yields the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Existence of isolated sunny lines)  \nFix a point \\(P=(p,q)\\in T_n\\). There exist infinitely many real numbers \\(m\\) such that the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{P,m}:\\;y-q=m(x-p)\n\\]\n\n* is sunny (\\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\));\n* contains no other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each distinct \\(Q=(a,b)\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\) the condition “\\(Q\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{P,m}\\)” forces a single slope  \n\n\\[\nm_{P,Q}=\\frac{b-q}{a-p}\\quad(\\text{with }m_{P,Q}=\\infty\\text{ if }a=p).\n\\]\n\nHence the finite set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{m_{P,Q}\\mid Q\\in T_n\\setminus\\{P\\}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains all slopes that either make the line non‑sunny or cause it to pass through another lattice point of \\(T_n\\).  \nChoosing any \\(m\\notin\\mathcal F\\) yields a sunny line through \\(P\\) that avoids every other point of \\(T_n\\). Since \\(\\mathcal F\\) is finite, infinitely many such \\(m\\) exist. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)  \nIn any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), at least two lines must be non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line is non‑sunny.  \nIf there is **no** non‑sunny line, then every line is sunny, i.e. has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nTwo distinct sunny lines intersect at a unique point.  \nThus \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover at most \\(\\binom{n}{2}+n\\) points (all intersection points together with the points lying on a single line).  \nFor \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}> \\binom{n}{2}+n =\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+n,\n\\]\n\nso not all points can be covered – contradiction.\n\nIf there is **exactly one** non‑sunny line, that line is parallel to either the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on this line must be covered by sunny lines.  \nBut the set of points not on the given non‑sunny line contains a complete copy of the triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\) (remove the outermost row or column depending on the direction of the non‑sunny line).  \nBy the previous paragraph, \\(T_{n-1}\\) cannot be covered by fewer than two sunny lines, contradicting the assumption that only one non‑sunny line is present. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, hence belong to the three families \\(\\mathcal H,\\mathcal V,\\mathcal D\\).  \nBy Lemma 3 we must have \\(n-k\\ge 2\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   P_t=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in T_n\n   \\]\n\n   (these are \\(k\\) distinct points on the hypotenuse \\(a+b=n+1\\)).  \n   By Lemma 2 choose a sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) through \\(P_t\\) that contains no other point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The slopes can be taken distinct, so the \\(\\ell_t\\) are distinct.  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_k=\\{\\ell_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two non‑sunny lines covering the rest.**  \n   Remove the \\(k\\) points \\(\\{P_t\\}\\) from \\(T_n\\); the remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=T_n\\setminus\\{P_t\\mid 1\\le t\\le k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(k\\le n-2\\), at least two distinct integers remain among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Choose any two distinct numbers \\(c_0,d_0\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with the additional requirement  \n\n   \\[\n   c_0\\neq n+1-d_0 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Such a pair exists: pick any \\(c_0\\) and then any \\(d_0\\neq n+1-c_0\\); the inequality is possible because \\(n\\ge3\\).)\n\n   Consider the horizontal line \\(H_{c_0}: y=c_0\\) and the vertical line \\(V_{d_0}: x=d_0\\).  \n   Both belong to the families \\(\\mathcal H\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\), hence are non‑sunny.\n\n   **Claim.** Every point of \\(U\\) lies on at least one of \\(H_{c_0}\\) or \\(V_{d_0}\\).  \n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in U\\). If \\(b=c_0\\) we are done.  \n   If \\(a=d_0\\) we are also done.  \n   Suppose neither holds. Then \\(b\\neq c_0\\) and \\(a\\neq d_0\\).  \n   Because \\((a,b)\\notin\\{P_t\\}\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \n   Hence \\(b=n+1-a\\ge n+1-d_0\\).  \n   The inequality (1) gives \\(c_0\\neq n+1-d_0\\); together with \\(b\\neq c_0\\) we obtain a contradiction to the fact that \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\).  \n   Therefore the assumption that both equalities fail is impossible, and the claim follows. ∎\n\n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N'=\\{\\,H_{c_0},\\,V_{d_0}\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Final family.**  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\mathcal S_k\\cup\\mathcal N'.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(k+2=n\\) distinct lines; exactly the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny, the two lines of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) are non‑sunny.  \n   By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\nThus for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\n- Lemma 3 shows that \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n- The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\nHence the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\] ∎"
