            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  The set that has to be covered  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Three families of non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal H &=\\{H_c:y=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal V &=\\{V_c:x=c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n\\},\\\\[2pt]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{D_s:x+y=s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Three covering families)  \n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in H_b,\\qquad (a,b)\\in V_a,\\qquad (a,b)\\in D_{a+b}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(1\\le b\\le n\\) we have \\(y=b\\); because \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) we have \\(x=a\\); and because \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(x+y=a+b\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe prove that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) must contain at least two non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A sunny line meets each diagonal at most once)  \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, then for distinct integers \\(s_1\\neq s_2\\) the intersections  \n\\(\\ell\\cap D_{s_1}\\) and \\(\\ell\\cap D_{s_2}\\) are distinct points; consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of each diagonal \\(D_s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo different diagonals are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\). A sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can intersect each diagonal in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\). Then \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at least two non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*  \n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nA sunny line can contain at most one of these three points (by Lemma 2, because the three points lie on three different diagonals). Hence at least two of the three covering lines must be non‑sunny.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and consider a covering \\(\\mathcal L\\) for \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nSuppose, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains at most one non‑sunny line.  \n\n*Case 1 – no non‑sunny line.*  \nAll lines are sunny, so each of them meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+2\\)) in at most one point (Lemma 2).  \nThere are \\(n+1\\) diagonals, each containing at least one point of \\(T_{n+1}\\); thus the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\((n+1)\\cdot (n+1)= (n+1)^2\\).  \nBut \\(|T_{n+1}|=\\frac{(n+1)(n+2)}2 > (n+1)\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case 2 – exactly one non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_0\\).*  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\ell_0\\) is horizontal, say \\(\\ell_0:H_c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nRemove from \\(T_{n+1}\\) all points lying on \\(\\ell_0\\); the remaining set is precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause the points with \\(b=c\\) are exactly those with maximal possible sum \\(n+2\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{\\ell_0\\}\\) must cover this set \\(T_n\\).  \nBy the induction hypothesis at least two of those \\(n\\) lines are non‑sunny, contradicting the assumption that \\(\\ell_0\\) was the only non‑sunny line.\n\nBoth cases are impossible; therefore any covering of \\(T_{n+1}\\) needs at least two non‑sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma 3 we obtain the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nStart with the family of all diagonal lines\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{D_s:x+y=s\\mid 2\\le s\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal D|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal D\\) alone covers every point of \\(T_n\\) (Lemma 1).  \nWe shall replace \\(k\\) of these diagonals by \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1 – choose the points to be “replaced”.**  \nFor \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,\\;n+1-t)\\in D_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nAll \\(P_t\\) lie on the same diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) and are distinct because \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n**Step 2 – sunny lines through the chosen points.**  \nFor each \\(t\\) apply Lemma 2’s corollary (the existence of a sunny line meeting a given diagonal in exactly one point) to obtain a sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\) that\n\n* passes through \\(P_t\\);\n* is not parallel to any of the three non‑sunny directions;\n* does **not** contain any other point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nSuch a line exists because the set of forbidden slopes (those that would make the line pass through another lattice point of \\(T_n\\) or become non‑sunny) is finite; choosing any other real slope yields the required line.  \nThe slopes can be