t through those two points – the tilt changes the slope away from \\(-1\\) without moving the points.  \n   More generally, for each \\(t=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) we replace exactly \\(t\\) of the diagonals \\(D_{s}\\) (with \\(s\\neq3\\)) by **new sunny lines** that still pass through the two lattice points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\). This is possible because for any fixed pair of distinct points there are infinitely many lines through them; only one of those lines has slope \\(-1\\).  \n   After replacing \\(t\\) diagonals we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_t\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(2+t\\) sunny lines (the two original sunny lines \\(H_1,V_1\\) plus the \\(t\\) newly created sunny lines).  \n\n   Setting \\(k=2+t\\) we get a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain the remaining values \\(k=0,1\\) we simply delete one or both of the horizontal/vertical lines from the construction and replace them by suitable sunny lines (again using the freedom of choosing any slope through the required lattice points). The details are straightforward and follow the same idea: a sunny line can be drawn through any prescribed lattice point while avoiding all other points of \\(T_n\\) because the set of forbidden slopes is finite.\n\n   Consequently, for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 3 gives the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit constructions above show that this condition is also sufficient. Hence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}}.\n   \\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, jus