n   \\bigl\\{\\,y = x + (j- k-1) + (n-k)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the last \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.\n\nSince we have exhibited constructions for **all** integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), these are precisely the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\displaystyle |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three directions\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{diagonal }(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence it lies on the line\n\\(x=a\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is\nsunny.  Consequently \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nT_n = T_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;S_k,\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-k}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le n-k\\},\n\\qquad\nS_k   = \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe two sets are disjoint and each of them is a right\u2011angled lattice triangle.\nThe first one, \\(T_{n-k}\\), has side\u2011length \\(n-k\\); the second one, \\(S_k\\), has\nside\u2011length \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering \\(T_{n-k}\\) with non\u2011sunny lines\n\nUse the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{n-k}\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), so it\nlies on one of the lines \\(V_i\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\nhence they are non\u2011sunny.  Thus the part \\(T_{n-k}\\) is covered by exactly\n\\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nTranslate \\(S_k\\) leftwards by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the image is the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid 1\\le a'\\le k,\\;1\\le b',\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt suffices to exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that contain all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x + (j-k-1).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_j\\) is not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  Set \\(d=b'-a'\\).  From the inequalities\n\\(1\\le b'\\le k+1-a'\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1-k\\le d\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the integer  \n\n\\[\nj = d + k + 1\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  Rearranging the definition of \\(j\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb' = a' + (j-k-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of the line \\(\\ell_j\\).  Hence \\((a',b')\\) lies on\n\\(\\ell_j\\).  \u220e\n\nNow translate the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  The resulting\n\\(k\\) lines are  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + (j-k-1) + (n-k)\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) is sunny, and by construction every point of the original set\n\\(S_k\\) belongs to one of the \\(L_j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Assembly of the whole configuration\n\nCollect the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_i\\) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_j\\):\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,y = x + (j-k-1)+(n-k)\\mid j=1,\\dots ,k\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal C\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; precisely the\nlast \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  By the arguments of Sections\u202f3 and\u202f4, every point\nof \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal C\\).\n\nThus for the chosen integer \\(k\\) we have exhibited a configuration satisfying the\ntwo required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Since we\nhave also shown that \\(k=0\\) is possible (and trivially \\(k=n\\) is obtained by\ntaking \\(k=n\\) in the construction), no other values of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All non\u2011negative integers }k\\text{ with }0\\le k\\le n\\text{ are\nexactly the admissible numbers.}}\n\\]"
