(n+1)/2}{\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor}\\ge n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence any family covering all points of \\(S\\) must contain at least \\(n\\) distinct\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for exactly one such \\(c\\); therefore the\n\\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) cover \\(S\\) completely.  \nEach \\(L_{c}\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.\nThus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n\\))\n\n1. **Choice of sums.**  \n   Select \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   c_{1},c_{2},\\dots ,c_{k}\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Selection of points.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) choose a point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\in S\\) with \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=c_{i}\\).  \n   This is always possible because for a given sum \\(c_{i}\\) there are \\(c_{i}-1\\ge1\\)\n   points in \\(S\\).\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) distinct real numbers  \n\n   \\[\n   m_{1},m_{2},\\dots ,m_{k}\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (e.g. \\(m_{i}=i\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y=m_{i}x+(b_{i}-m_{i}a_{i})\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By construction \\(L_{i}\\) passes through \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\).  \n   Because the slopes \\(m_{i}\\) are pairwise different, the lines \\(L_{i}\\) are\n   distinct.  None of the slopes equals \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or\n   \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.\n\n4. **Remaining points.**  \n   Remove the \\(k\\) points \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) from \\(S\\).  \n   The sums of the remaining points are precisely the set  \n\n   \\[\n   C'=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{c_{1},\\dots ,c_{k}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains exactly \\(n-k\\) elements.\n\n5. **Non‑sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(c\\in C'\\) introduce the line  \n\n   \\[\n   M_{c}:\\;x+y=c .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(S\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\) lie on \\(M_{c}\\); thus the\n   \\(n-k\\) lines \\(\\{M_{c}\\mid c\\in C'\\}\\) cover all points of \\(S\\) that are not\n   already covered by the sunny lines.\n\n6. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\{M_{c}\\mid c\\in C'\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, covers every\n   point of \\(S\\), and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_{i}\\)’s).\n\nThus for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Final conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* At least \\(n\\) lines are necessary (inequality (3)).\n* For every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is an explicit construction of exactly\n  \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\)\n  sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
