th \\(X_{c}\\) and \\(Y_{t-c}\\) are parallel respectively to the \\(x\\)-axis and the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are not sunny.  \nThe set of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that lie on \\(L_{t}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{n}\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in P_{t}\\) we have either \\(a=c\\) or \\(b=t-c\\) (but not both, because \\(c\\neq t-c\\)).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nP_{t}\\subseteq X_{c}\\cup Y_{t-c},\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nand the two lines in (7) together cover exactly the same points of \\(T_{n}\\) as the original diagonal line \\(L_{t}\\).\n\nConsequently, if a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) satisfies (C) and contains the line \\(L_{t}\\), we may replace \\(L_{t}\\) by the two lines \\(X_{c},Y_{t-c}\\) and the covering condition (C) remains true.  The number of lines increases by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Introducing a sunny line while preserving the total number of lines\n\nStarting from \\(\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) we now describe a *single* operation that\n\n* adds **one** sunny line,\n* keeps the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\),\n* preserves condition (C).\n\nLet \\(L_{t}\\in\\mathcal{L}_{0}\\) be a diagonal line that has not yet been altered, and choose a number \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le t-1\\).  \nPerform the following three steps:\n\n1. **Split** \\(L_{t}\\) into the two axis‑parallel lines \\(X_{c},Y_{t-c}\\) as in (7).  \n   The family now contains \\(n+1\\) lines and still satisfies (C).\n\n2. **Insert** a sunny line \\(S\\) of any slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (for instance \\(S:\\;y=mx\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\)).  \n   The family now has \\(n+2\\) lines; condition (C) is unchanged because the new line does not pass through any point of \\(T_{n}\\) (its slope is chosen so that it avoids all lattice points of the triangle).\n\n3. **Delete** one of the two axis‑parallel lines introduced in step 1, say the one with the larger constant term (i.e. 