angle of side‑length \\(n-k\\); \\(S_k\\) is a right‑angled lattice triangle of side‑length \\(k\\) located in the upper‑right corner of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering \\(T_{n-k}\\) with non‑sunny lines\n\nUse the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEach point of \\(T_{n-k}\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\), so it lies on one of the \\(V_i\\).  All \\(V_i\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis and therefore non‑sunny.  Hence \\(T_{n-k}\\) is covered by exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering \\(S_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nTranslate \\(S_k\\) leftwards by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\); the image is  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a',b')\\mid 1\\le a'\\le k,\\;1\\le b',\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x + (j-k-1).\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_j\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny (it is not horizontal, vertical, nor parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n**Claim.** Every lattice point of \\(T_k\\) lies on some \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a',b')\\in T_k\\).  Set \\(d=b'-a'\\).  From \\(1\\le b'\\le k+1-a'\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n1-k\\le d\\le k-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the integer  \n\n\\[\nj = d + k + 1\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).  Rearranging the definition of \\(j\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb' = a' + (j-k-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(\\ell_j\\).  Hence \\((a',b')\\) lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).  ∎\n\nNow translate the lines \\(\\ell_j\\) back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  The resulting lines are  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y = x + (j-n-1),\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,k.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_j\\) is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) we have \\(a' = a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b'=b\\); by the claim \\((a',b')\\) lies on some \\(\\ell_j\\), which translates to 